chapter_2_38a71f0e		The strange object seemed almost drawn to Nabisin. {w}It shook a little, though that could’ve been Shelibuum’s nervousness getting the best of her.	game/chapter-2.rpy	196	"[what]"
chapter_2_668c2d9e		The figurine had no imperfections, bumps, or signs of knife work, either.	game/chapter-2.rpy	197	"[what]"
chapter_2_8ce4b2de		It has been carved with perfect precision.	game/chapter-2.rpy	198	"[what]"
chapter_2_704f6977		Examining the object further, Nabisin thought of asking Shelibuum, but she looked into Nabisin’s eyes, which mirrored his soul.	game/chapter-2.rpy	199	"[what]"
chapter_2_b43c3abd		Signaling the guards she was done, they quickly scolded her back to outside.	game/chapter-2.rpy	200	"[what]"
chapter_2_e8af8e37		Eluded by the statuette’s meaning, or the lack thereof, he pondered who could carve so perfectly like that.	game/chapter-2.rpy	201	"[what]"
chapter_2_ca0845c0		The statuette was made of wood, with strange metal intertwining the core and then surfacing in intricate patterns.	game/chapter-2.rpy	202	"[what]"
chapter_2_da95aeb2		Maybe another language, Nabisin thought.	game/chapter-2.rpy	203	"[what]"
chapter_2_9ccb7616		Putting the statuette away for a second, he tried to think of many solutions to the enigma.	game/chapter-2.rpy	204	"[what]"
chapter_2_45cf02aa		Suddenly he recognized it; that was Lulu’s art sculpted into a beautiful statuette!	game/chapter-2.rpy	205	"[what]"
chapter_2_45e819c2		The perfection was beyond any other man’s capacity, only one such as Lulu could’ve made such beautiful statuette with such detail and mindfulness.	game/chapter-2.rpy	206	"[what]"
chapter_2_87f42bbe		It was a goat, like him, with its arms outstretched in prayer formation, though now that he examined the statuette, it seemed malleable enough to form any pose.	game/chapter-2.rpy	207	"[what]"
chapter_2_07d6b41a		A visible smile cracked onto his face. Nabisin could feel as if the dark corridor he had been so desperately looking for a way out from had become less narrow.	game/chapter-2.rpy	208	"[what]"
chapter_2_6b734c61	n	{i}I knew it!{/i}	game/chapter-2.rpy	209	n "[what]"
chapter_2_f256f08f	n	{i}He’s still after me!{/i}	game/chapter-2.rpy	210	n "[what]"
chapter_2_4bb5bee1		Even though happiness restored Nabisin’s hope, as if he could sense Lulu’s presence in his enclosure, reaching for his chin and caressing his fur, he had more pressing matters.	game/chapter-2.rpy	211	"[what]"
chapter_2_b6cfc792		Getting a sign is the first step towards his liberation. {w}Now, the hard part is still to come. He attempted to script how his trial may proceed in his mind.	game/chapter-2.rpy	212	"[what]"
chapter_2_97d97abe		Now that the heavy weight was swiftly lifted, the bed seemed to draw even closer to him. {w}His thoughts were all around the place.	game/chapter-2.rpy	213	"[what]"
chapter_2_1d38b03b		It gave himself another excuse to just lay down and forget everything.	game/chapter-2.rpy	214	"[what]"
chapter_2_8782a478		The chilly night felt a bit warmer that day. {w}The fine, scented breeze reminded Nabisin of his time sleeping at the temple headquarters once he had been initiated as a priest.	game/chapter-2.rpy	215	"[what]"
chapter_2_4a882ecf		He was required to live by older priests and his colleagues. {w}A certain someone always tried to bother him at every turn.	game/chapter-2.rpy	216	"[what]"
chapter_2_dbc0df89		Speaking up to the higher ones was of no avail, as they seemed to enjoy seeing acolytes being harsh to each other.	game/chapter-2.rpy	217	"[what]"
chapter_2_d673442f		Sometimes it could be fun. {w}They used to play many silly games, but even when harsher forms of aggression took form, higher-ups still didn’t care.	game/chapter-2.rpy	218	"[what]"
chapter_2_8466a1f4		The thick veil of the night was lifted, and sunlight entered through the small crevice in his cell of involuntary imprisonment.	game/chapter-2.rpy	219	"[what]"
chapter_2_cfe0d665		Every day he followed the same routine, tiresome as it was. {w}He only had one thing to do: wake up and face the guards.	game/chapter-2.rpy	220	"[what]"
chapter_2_203a25d4		He shouldn’t have become accustomed to being in such a dire state, but he confessed he did out of fear he wouldn’t make it out.	game/chapter-2.rpy	221	"[what]"
chapter_2_d81ce300		Still, yesterday’s spark of change gave Nabisin a reason to hold out long enough for another sigh.	game/chapter-2.rpy	222	"[what]"
chapter_2_ea2c0bf7		Each at their respective spots, the two guards shuffled places. {w}Nabisin assumed much was to come in the following hours.	game/chapter-2.rpy	223	"[what]"
chapter_2_6a30787b		The rusty creak of the cell opening would have been deafening to Nabisin had he not been so anxious about what was going to happen.	game/chapter-2.rpy	224	"[what]"
chapter_2_9e77be33		Being scolded down the corridors that served as his imprisonment, he felt several gushes of air coming his way, damp and breezy. The incense of nearby temples was noticeable to such a degree Nabisin knew today must be the god Shamash’s birthday.	game/chapter-2.rpy	225	"[what]"
chapter_2_0d81894c		A weird stinging sensation across his arms made them incredibly itchy for a moment. {w}His fur felt as if being torn apart by small flames, and his lower legs also felt worse than painful stings.	game/chapter-2.rpy	226	"[what]"
chapter_2_60ac11e2		Then, as if nothing had happened, relief overwhelmed him. {w}The winds settled on him, making it much easier to reassess the situation and check his arms and legs.	game/chapter-2.rpy	227	"[what]"
chapter_2_20c3494b		Unsurprisingly, nothing seemed etched into his fur, as the burning sensation would have indicated. {w}Not a single scratch or patch of fur had been damaged.	game/chapter-2.rpy	228	"[what]"
chapter_2_5e34b408		Was his mind playing some sick trick on him on the way to his trial? Had he done something unusual?	game/chapter-2.rpy	229	"[what]"
chapter_2_1386af9f		Trying to calm himself, Nabisin remembered the small statuette tucked in his dirty robes.	game/chapter-2.rpy	230	"[what]"
chapter_2_f2e6a6d3		It was the same as yesterday, though some of its metal markings seemed to glisten more than before, reflecting the sun like a crystalline pond.	game/chapter-2.rpy	231	"[what]"
chapter_2_c2f361da		Still trying to decode the meaning of these strange series of events, the statuette felt lighter for a second, as if could move on its own.	game/chapter-2.rpy	232	"[what]"
chapter_2_2af809e5		Then its usual weight came back.	game/chapter-2.rpy	233	"[what]"
chapter_2_a8c287a4		If the gods wish to blind me, they can do as they please, but this absurdity needs to end. What in the name of heavens has happened to me?	game/chapter-2.rpy	234	"[what]"
chapter_2_cc1a72da		Had my mind turned into stew for straying away from the gods!	game/chapter-2.rpy	235	"[what]"
chapter_2_423d0d12		Cursing under his breath, Nabisin was fuming, insecure about his situation, and even more nervous about being falsely accused of a crime he hadn’t committed.	game/chapter-2.rpy	236	"[what]"
chapter_2_8d17971b		Before long, the palace shadow projected onto Nabisin and the guards scolding him. {w}This menacing construction was the House of the Just and where Shulgi, King of the Four Corners of the World resided together with his personnel.	game/chapter-2.rpy	237	"[what]"
chapter_2_38afc40d		He once visited this place during his years of learning as an acolyte; now he is here as a prisoner.	game/chapter-2.rpy	238	"[what]"
chapter_2_909e4988		The place was mostly the same as it always had been; nothing new was here beyond the new guards and King Shulgi sitting on his throne.	game/chapter-2.rpy	242	"[what]"
chapter_2_c9b230c9		The trial would begin once the scribes arrived at the scene, prompted to either record this legal procedure or to spew and remind the accused person of his crimes.	game/chapter-2.rpy	243	"[what]"
chapter_2_0306d295		Neither Lulu nor Shelibuum were seen. They were either late or were not invited. {w}This seemed quite strange, given that they also partook in Nabisin’s “crime” so to speak.	game/chapter-2.rpy	244	"[what]"
chapter_2_8e421bbb		Out of the corner of his eye, a most unpleasant visage appeared: that of Utudug, the bastard who had made Nabisin’s life a torment. Hadn’t the universe paired them during his time as an acolyte, things could have been considerably different.	game/chapter-2.rpy	246	"[what]"
chapter_2_2a238864		Trying to control his breath, Nabisin hated Utudug with every fur of his being; he felt more than willing to strangle him and leave him to rot under the Euphrates; may the gods forget he was ever there.	game/chapter-2.rpy	247	"[what]"
chapter_2_04105866		The silent hatred behind his facade intoxicated Nabisin’s hot, boiling blood with the most vile poison, so much so that he was crushing Lulu’s gift he had been holding this entire time.	game/chapter-2.rpy	248	"[what]"
